---
import type { ArticleMetadata } from "#lib/articles";

import { Body } from "#lib/elements";
import { Article, WrapImage } from "#lib/components";

import image from "#lib/articles/2026-09-16-everything-is-changing-and-i-cant-keep-up/000.avif";
import subject from "#lib/articles/2026-09-16-everything-is-changing-and-i-cant-keep-up/Subject.png";
import subject2 from "#lib/articles/2026-09-16-everything-is-changing-and-i-cant-keep-up/Subject2.png";

export const metadata: ArticleMetadata = {
	title: "Everything Is Changing and I Can't Keep Up!",
	description: "",
	date: {
		published: "2026-09-16",
		modified: "2026-09-16"
	},
	author: "Celia Joliffe",
	category: "words",
	image
};
---

<Body title={metadata.title} description={metadata.description} image={metadata.image}>
	<main class="spread">
		<Article {...metadata}>
			<h1 class="help">I wanted to combine two things: sportswear & NYC to create a relaxed city look</h1>
		</Article>

		<Article {...metadata}>
			<p>
				I would love to say I journal every day, but this couldn't be further from the truth.
				In actual fact, I journaled for the first time in four years early this summer (and haven't picked up the designated 2016 Claire's notebook since).
				I like to say it's because my life has become very busy, but A) I definitely do still have ten minutes to write something, and B) I realise I force my life to be busy so I don't have to feel the nostalgia of so many chapters closing in such quick succession.
			</p>

			<WrapImage src={subject} height="700px" />

			<!-- <img style="height: 1000px; width: auto;" src={subject.src} /> -->

			<p>
				The prompt that popped up while I was scrolling was: 'Write about a room you will never enter again.'
				For some reason, this encapsulated everything I had been avoiding thinking about, and I found myself sat with the (previously mentioned) 2016 Claire's notebook, writing pages of teary thoughts.
				I have lived in five houses/apartments since moving out.
				At eighteen, I walked completely petrified into an absolutely tiny Austrian town for a couple of weeks, which subsequently turned into a couple of months, and now a couple of years.
				I walked out having had the most wonderful (and chaotic) experience, and today it's been four years.
				I have blinked, and the babies have grown into amazing, walking, talking children.
				I have learnt so much from this lovely family.
				I arrived terrified and insecure, and through many comfort-pushing experiences (notably, one weekend off, arriving alone to Kraków at 2am with no battery and a stolen credit card), I felt I walked out as a brand new person: able to speak up for myself and move with real confidence for the first time ever.
			</p>
		</Article>
	</main>
</Body>

<style>
	main {
		background-color: #5F4236;
	}

	p {
		color: white;
	}

	.help {

	}
</style>
